{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Or let him take hold.</b> Israel, by my strength — then I will not fight against YHWH, like the sense 'YHWH was like an enemy' (Lamentations 2:5)." "And the meaning of <b>let him make peace with me</b> is: at times, and not with the nations of the world for their sake." "And Rabbi Moses the Karaite said that its meaning is thus — this one who says [it], I do not say [it] in the <b>wrath</b> that I have. <b>Oh that I had.</b> Like 'who would give to me,' and like it 'for a land of the Negev you have given me' (Judges 1:15) — who would give me the brier and the thorn in the vineyard, and the meaning is: if Israel does not remove the wicked from among them, I will kill all. <b>Or let him take hold of my strength.</b> Then Jacob will strike root." "And in my opinion, <b>I have no wrath</b> is not the words of HaShem. And do not wonder, for in this book there are many such [passages] — 'until now we were in labor' (Isaiah 26:18), 'your dead shall live' (Isaiah 26:19), and these are the words of the man of Judah. After HaShem said, 'I YHWH guard it moment by moment, lest [harm] befall it,' he said: We do not speak in wrath, for if the enemy comes to set brier and thorn in the vineyard, I would kill myself — like 'Ezekiel shall be a sign to you' (Ezekiel 24:24), and Samuel (1 Samuel 12:11), 'as he did on this day' (Leviticus 8:34). And behold, in the matter of war he spoke with 'Oh that I had'; or let HaShem strengthen, that He give me the strength of a covenant, that HaShem be at peace with me, and the enemy too. Then Jacob will strike root. And I needed this interpretation because of the word 'let him make peace with me,' for HaShem makes peace alone."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {2 "Resolved הכהן as 'the Karaite' following the standard identification of Ibn Ezra's interlocutor R. Moses ha-Kohen as Moses the Karaite.", 3 "The root לחם appears twice in comment [3] with different senses: להרג את עצמי renders the idiom where the speaker would 'fight against himself'; the first occurrence 'in the matter of war' (במלחמה) is literal. Also resolved the three consecutive biblical fragments from Isaiah 26 as Ibn Ezra's evidence for non-divine speech in this book."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:55:32.648800104Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}